<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/10th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Torrevicente</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">981</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Battle of San Vicente</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Torrevicente was fought in 981 in the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soria">Soria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, between the Córdoban general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almanzor">Almanzor</a> and his father-in-law Ghalib, who had rebelled against him with the support of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamplona">Pamplona</a>. Ghalib died during the fighting and his army was routed, leaving Almanzor as the unchallenged master of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliphate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>. Ramiro Garcés, king of Viguera, is said to have died in the battle.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The battle set two policies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliphate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> against each other. Galib ibn Abd al-Rahman wished to continue the course of the caliphs Abd ar-Rahman III and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-hakam-ii">al-Hakam II</a>, keeping supremacy over the Christian principalities in peace. Ibn Abi Amir, later known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almanzor">Almanzor</a>, was pursuing a new policy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a>, marked by seven attacks on the Christians in the previous three years. Galib was joined by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramiro-garces-of-viguera">Ramiro Garcés of Viguera</a> and Count García Fernández of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>.</p><p>The main accounts are all Arabic: Ibn al-Khatib, al-Udri and Ibn Hazm, who relied on his father Ahmad ibn Hazm, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> who fought on Ibn Abi Amir's side. No Christian source mentions the battle.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Ibn Abi Amir left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> on 11 May 981. On 7 July the two armies met before the castle of San Vicente, probably Torrevicente near Atienza, as agreed beforehand. Friday passed quietly and fighting began on Saturday, 9 July. The octogenarian Galib himself led a charge that broke the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berbers</a> of Ibn Abi Amir's right wing, and the left wing broke too.</p><p>Galib then rode into a nearby ravine, where his companions later found him dead beside his calm horse, of an unknown cause. Taking his death as a sign from God, many of his followers sought peace, bringing his seal, his hand and his horse as proof. The caliphal army then inflicted a severe defeat on the Christian allies and pursued Galib's remaining troops towards Atienza. García escaped, and Muslim historians treated the battle as a victory over the Christians.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Ibn Abi Amir went on to take Atienza and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calatayud">Calatayud</a>, the centres of Galib's support, and raided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>. Muslim sources called this twelfth of his campaigns the Campaign of the Victory, and on returning to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> he took the honorific al-Mansur bi-llah, victorious through God, from which the name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almanzor">Almanzor</a> comes. Galib's skin, stuffed with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a>, was crucified at the gate of the alcázar and his head displayed at al-Zahira. Scholars still debate the identity of the Ramiro said to have died, since some date Ramiro Garcés's death to 991.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Torrevicente” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ramiro is said to have died at the <b>Battle of Torrevicente</b> in 981.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-viguera">Kingdom of Viguera</a></li></ul></section>
